f 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158690 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158690 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "64902e3c-53ad-5d5a-a122-1bd9b485f300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158691 #edb/ref 1080 "f_{Y}(y) = {8e^{-8y},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158691 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158691 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a39d6df3-4e4a-5932-a1af-4e0f960c190a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158692 #edb/ref 1080 "f_{Y}(y) = {2e^{-2y},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158692 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158692 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b0871774-b1e2-5596-8d00-8ff73ed2f061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158693 #edb/ref 1080 "f_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{1}{2}e^{-4y},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158693 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158693 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bec17b16-7ddb-518e-b615-9860111fb70a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158694 #edb/ref 1080 "f_{Y}(y) = {2e^{-4y},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158694 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158694 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d263f88d-7fa5-5478-a3a2-09700c40f738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158695 #edb/ref 1080 "f_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{1}{4}e^{-4y},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158695 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158695 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7cde2501-ad66-5893-b560-ce623dbaff40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161846 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161846 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n${2x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nIf $Y =1-3X$, then what is the probability density function of $Y$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161846 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6276f196-80de-58b7-acb6-a715ee955f6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161846 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y$, where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X$, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X$, where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X$, respectively.\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y$.\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = 1-3X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X$. Moreover, for $0 < x < 1$, we have $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bb641b1920d0ded33e4ffa25f3e0af856550dd3820edf30416bd79c3a6da264.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y$, for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(1 - 3X \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(-3X \\le y - 1) \\\\\n&= P(X \\ge \\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= 1 - F_{X}(\\frac{1 - y}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1$, we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= F_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\\n&= 0 - (-\\frac{1}{3}) \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(\\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot f_{X}(\\frac{1 - y}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot f_{X}(\\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{9}(1 - y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{2}{9}(1 - y),\\begin{vmatrix}-2 < y < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161847 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9074" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161847 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n${\\frac{3}{8}(2 - x)^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nIf $Y = (2-X)^3$, then what is the probability density function of $Y$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161847 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9f862ab-0544-503e-8627-3de73ec60594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161847 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y$, where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X$, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X$, where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X$, respectively.\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y$.\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = (2-X)^3$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X$. Moreover, for $0 \\lt x \\lt 2$, we have $0 \\lt y \\lt 8$.\n\n![](images/2c/2c07ffe5af282ba1689cb6f49201b979afb4186d0128dd6a97f446b7240d76d6.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y$, for $0 \\lt y \\lt 8$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P((2 - X)^{3} \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(2 - X \\le y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= P(-X \\le y^{1/3} - 2) \\\\\n&= P(X \\ge 2 - y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= 1 - F_{X}(2 - y^{1/3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $0 \\lt y \\lt 8$, we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= F_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\\n&= 0 - (-\\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}}) \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(2 - y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot f_{X}(2 - y^{1/3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot f_{X}(2 - y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(2 - (2 - y^{1/3}))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(2 - 2 + y^{1/3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(y^{1/3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{8},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < y < 8 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162046 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162046 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162046 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162046 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162046 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162046 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162046 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162046 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2008912a-15de-5248-a9b5-c375eb3dc292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162047 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "93f1f64d-e88e-5f60-8655-459b15eaf715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162191 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162191 #edb/ref 1045 "The Distribution Function Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162191 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162191 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162191 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "94bfdff9-fb3b-5c84-9048-5e2a573a36a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162248 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162248 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162248 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n$f_{X}(x) = {4e^{-4x},\\begin{vmatrix}x > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nIf $Y = 2X$, then what is the probability density function of $Y$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162248 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80402a14-4dfc-5a15-8269-ddd0f957785c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162953 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162953 #edb/ref 1061 33485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162953 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "545a2e11-b3d3-5c57-9ddf-9aec604aff0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162953 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162957 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162957 #edb/ref 1061 33840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162957 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e0e38c10-faac-57fb-b114-7d58e7238835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162957 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162958 #edb/ref 1061 33841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162958 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec2fc57f-ed17-5553-9197-3e0d2cd11097" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162958 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163098 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163098 #edb/ref 1061 38639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163098 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b31cb7f-dcfe-50f4-9a5b-26957ca91f13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163098 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163263 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable whose probability density function is known. Suppose we want to define a new random variable $Y$ as\n\n$Y = u(X)$,\nwhere $u = u(X)$ is a function of $X$. How do we find the probability density function of $Y$?\n\nTo find the PDF of $Y$, we can use the **distribution function method**. The distribution function method consists of two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X$, where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X$, respectively.\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y$ of $Y$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll assume that the function $u$ is strictly monotonic (i.e., strictly increasing or decreasing). The case where $u$ is not monotonic will be dealt with in a future lesson.\n\nLet's see an example of the distribution function method in action." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163263 #edb/ref 1065 5355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163263 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6e59563a-5641-533c-8434-d07d587129e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163263 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163339 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose the continuous random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n${e^{-x},\\begin{vmatrix}x > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nLet\n\n$$\nY= 1+2X\n$$\n\nLet's use the distribution function method to find the probability density function of $Y$.\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\nY = 1+2X\n$$\n\nis a strictly increasing function of $X$. Moreover, for $x > 0$, we have $y > 1$.\n\n![](images/54/544e551755f4c8a575ef227b77fe520deec203f08654eec31109e496141aa91c.png)\n\nLet's consider $y > 1$. We write down the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y$, and then use our transformation to isolate $X$ in the parentheses. This will allow us to write $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(2X + 1 \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(2X \\le y - 1) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le \\frac{y - 1}{2}) \\\\\n&= F_{X}(\\frac{y - 1}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $y > 1$, we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= F_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dy}(F_{X}(\\frac{y - 1}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dy}(\\frac{y - 1}{2}) \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y - 1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y - 1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot f_{X}(\\frac{y - 1}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot f_{X}(\\frac{y - 1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot e^{- (y - 1)/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}e^{(1 - y)/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= {\\frac{1}{2}e^{(1 - y)/2},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy (and often worthwhile) to check that this is a valid probability density function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163339 #edb/ref 1065 6387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163339 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6eea312a-622e-5ce1-ae71-45e4cc089144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163339 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
